{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are reasonably calibrated in days 1–10, but become increasingly too low beyond ~day 20, especially in rising regimes. Apply an upward drift adjustment that grows with horizon (e.g., +0.05 to +0.15 EUR/day after day 15 when trend is up).",
    "Trend/lag error: the model under-reacts to late-stage rallies (true series accelerates upward while forecasts flatten or decline). If the last 5–10 days of history show positive momentum, increase the slope of the forecast path (add a momentum term) rather than reverting to the mean.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts too aggressively after moderate pullbacks, leading to underestimation of subsequent continuation moves. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is elevated and the 10–20d trend is positive.",
    "Spike handling: after upside breaks (new local highs), the model tends to cap prices near ~68–70 while realized moves extend to 72–76. Introduce a breakout regime: if price is making 20–30d highs, widen the upside band and shift the forecast distribution upward (raise cap/ceiling).",
    "Asymmetry: downside periods (mid-horizon dips toward ~64–66) are captured better than upside extensions; corrections should be asymmetric—primarily add upside adjustment in bullish regimes rather than symmetric widening."
  ]
}
